thread:
maria_support: FYI customer (AcmeRetail) reporting embedding failures and weird dims after upgrading to our TS SDK v0.9.2. Error: "embedding dimension mismatch: expected 1536 got 768". They say it started after bumping model to "embed-large-v1" and enabling batching.

alex_eng: Can you paste the exact request payload / headers they send? Is this on Hosted or Dedicated?

maria_support: Hosted. Customer supplied this snippet from their production logs (redacted):

maria_support: ```json
{
  "model": "embed-large-v1",
  "input": ["product description...", "short title..."],
  "batch_size": 32,
  "client": "redwood-ts@0.9.2"
}
```

maria_support: And the server side error they get: `400 Bad Request - embedding dimension mismatch: expected 1536 got 768`

samir_ml: Two quick hypotheses: 1) They accidentally sent embeddings produced from a different model (embed-small-v1 dim=768) to an index expecting 1536. 2) Our TS SDK batching code is flattening nested arrays when it serializes, causing elements to be truncated or batched incorrectly.

jessica_sdk: I briefly looked at the v0.9.2 release notes — we changed the internal batch concat path to optimize memory. There's a known corner-case where typed arrays (Float32Array) can be serialized as base64 in some bundlers. Could be related.

alex_eng: Do we have the customer's repo snippet? Any chance they precompute embeddings locally (e.g., using a different model) then push to our ingest API?

maria_support: Customer says they only call our /embeddings endpoint from Node. No precompute elsewhere. They provided a minimized repro: `node repro.js` that calls our TS client. They claim it fails 1/3 of the time when batch_size>1, works reliably with batch_size=1.

samir_ml: That pattern strongly suggests a batching serialization bug. When batch_size=1 path uses single-object code path; multi-object uses the new fast path.

jessica_sdk: I asked for the repro and ran it locally on mac. Repro succeeds for me on v0.9.2 when I force node v18. But I can reproduce dimension mismatch when I bundle with webpack and target webworker — Float32Array turns into an ArrayBuffer slice and JSON.stringify yields an object shape that our backend interprets as a shorter vector.

maria_support: The customer is on Node 20 in production and uses esbuild to bundle their lambda.

alex_eng: Action items: 1) Ask customer to try SDK v0.9.3-rc (we built a patch that reverts the fast concat) OR switch to server-side only (unbundled) Node. 2) Add server-side validation & clearer error message. 3) Patch ingest path to detect typed-array encodings.

jessica_sdk: Patch 0.9.3-rc link: https://pkg.redwood.dev/redwood-ts@0.9.3-rc — includes a fix: normalizeEmbeddingsToArray(emb) before JSON.stringify. Example TS fix snippet I asked them to try:

jessica_sdk: ```ts
import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
const inputs = ["a", "b", "c"];
const res = await client.embeddings.create({ model: 'embed-large-v1', input: inputs, batch_size: 16 });
// If using a bundler, ensure embeddings are plain number[] not Float32Array
```

maria_support: Customer tried 0.9.3-rc, reports much fewer failures but still occasional 400s under heavy throughput. They also report timeouts: their worker times out after 5s waiting for our response (they use Promise.all with 64 concurrent requests).

samir_ml: That points to secondary issue: throughput/backpressure. If they spawn too many concurrent embedding requests, our routing may route some sub-batches to fallback smaller model variants or hit rate limiters causing partial responses. We should check whether those 400s correlate with spikes in their qps.

alex_eng: Can we confirm server logs for their API key around the timestamps they provided (2026-02-12T14:02:xxZ range)? Also check if any model aliasing happened (embed-large-v1 pointed to a different checkpoint with 768 dim for a short window).

maria_support: I pulled logs — there was a brief deploy window where embed-large-v1 was aliased to embed-large-quant for testing; quantized variant yields internally 768 dims because of truncation in our preprocessor. A deploy-bot message is in infra logs at deploy 2026-02-12T14:00:12Z.

deploy-bot: Deployer: @infra-release — rolled alias embed-large-v1 -> embed-large-quant for canary traffic 2026-02-12T14:00:12Z (ID: canary-456).

alex_eng: There is the smoking gun. During canary we accidentally sent that alias to some hosted customers. We need to: 1) Roll alias back (done?), 2) Patch aliasing gating, 3) Refund/credit affected customers, and 4) add automated dimension mismatch alarms in console.

samir_ml: For immediate customer workaround: ask them to pin model to explicit checksum name (not alias) e.g., `embed-large-v1@sha256:abcdef...` or downgrade to embed-small-v1 if they need guaranteed 768. Also recommend they set batch_size<=8 and use exponential backoff with jitter for retries. Their client snippet for backoff (JS):

samir_ml: ```ts
async function withRetry(fn, retries=5) {
  for (let i=0;i<retries;i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (e) {
      const wait = Math.min(1000 * 2**i, 5000) + Math.random()*200;
      await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r, wait));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('max retries');
}
```

jessica_sdk: Also recommend they upgrade to 0.9.3 non-rc when we cut it. The fix for bundlers will be in that patch.

maria_support: Customer accepted workaround: pin model checksum and set batch_size=8 + retry. They asked about Go and Python SDKs — could they hit similar issues?

jessica_sdk: Python and Go SDKs don't use that fast JS concat path, so unlikely to see the bundler/Float32Array issue. But they can hit timeout/retry/backpressure problems. Example Go snippet to set context timeout + retries:

jessica_sdk: ```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 10*time.Second)
defer cancel()
for i:=0;i<3;i++ {
  _, err := client.Embeddings.Create(ctx, opts)
  if err == nil { break }
  time.Sleep(time.Duration(math.Pow(2,float64(i))) * 200*time.Millisecond)
}
```

alex_eng: Next steps for us (internal):
- eng-runtime: add strict dimension check earlier and emit metric `embedding_dimension_mismatch` (P1)
- eng-release: tighten alias rollout gating and a hotfix to prevent alias -> quantized toggles for live traffic (P0)
- sdk: cut 0.9.3 stable and publish notes about bundlers + typed arrays (P1)
- customer success: offer credit and help pin model checksum (maria)

maria_support: I've opened ticket RW-7642 and CC'd infra-release, eng-runtime, and samir_ml. Customer is satisfied with temporary pins and retry guidance but wants ETA for stable SDK & infra fix.

samir_ml: I'll own the runtime metric work. ETA: 48 hours to get dimension checks live, 72 hours for alias gating fix.

jessica_sdk: I'll schedule the 0.9.3 cut today and follow up here when published.

maria_support: Thanks everyone. I'll keep the thread updated. Customer to be notified once we roll alias changes and SDK stable. :thumbsup:

alex_eng: Great. Add postmortem note if credits > $X. Also add suggested docs changes: "Embedding dimension mismatch" troubleshooting section and bundler guidance.

maria_support: Noted. Closing interim loop with customer; keeping thread open until all fixes are live.
